  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none noted",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests whether downstream Verilog-generation success changes with inference-time parallel sample count, using Hit@k rather than held-out pretraining loss or model-size scaling.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports results for parallel inference sampling, not the claimed parameter/token/compute power laws. Only downstream task success rates bear on V6. Results about API cost, simulation throughput, code dispersion, and cited results are not rows for the tested version.",
    "test_reason": "The study scales inference-time parallel sample count (Hit@k, 1–512 samples) on fixed API models. It does not vary parameters, tokens or training compute, and does not measure pretraining loss. V6 is about downstream gains with model or training scale or pretraining loss, so sample-count gains measure a different construct (test-time scaling). Hoffmann et al. is cited only to say training-time scaling has slowed.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. Authors conclude that parallel sampling to hundreds of samples is cost-effective and beats prior Verilog results. Gains come from output diversity, are bounded by model capability on hard problems, and are shaped by temperature. There are no claims about pretraining scaling laws.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Measures test-time parallel sampling and functional code success, not pretraining loss across parameter, token, or training-compute sweeps.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Parallel-sampling gains vary with benchmark and problem difficulty. For the two Gemini models, the authors report temperature-dependent diversity and success/convergence; Gemini-2.0-flash shows no improvement beyond five samples at T = 0.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Hit@k on three Verilog benchmarks rises with parallel sample count (1→512) for six API models: +4.7 to +24 points. Gains taper; authors describe a sub-logarithmic trend (c ∝ log log N). Gains are limited on the hardest problems and plateau early at low temperature.",
        "proposed_claim": "Test-time repeated-sampling coverage grows with sample count, roughly log-like with diminishing returns (inference-compute scaling).",
